Entire Explain:

The intent architecture is a two-stage pipeline:

```text
input / AI / callbacks
        ↓
intent components on actor
        ↓
intent validation
        ↓
canonical intent systems
        ↓
movement / combat / spell / interaction effects
```

The scheduler runs this as:

1. `ai` — systems propose intents.
2. `intents` — intents are validated and consumed.
3. `effects` — damage reactions, cooldowns, regeneration, statuses.
4. `cleanup` — removal and spatial synchronization.

The central wiring lives in [`scheduler.js`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/main/scheduler.js:210).

### How the main intents work

`MoveIntent`

Created by player input and many AI systems. It contains only direction plus cancellation metadata. [`movementSystem`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/rules/systems/movementSystem.js:165) validates terrain, status effects, blocking, confusion, encumbrance, and speed, then consumes it.

It is the most contested intent in the game. Chase, scurry, social aggro, townfolk, summoned creatures, pets, and others can all produce it.

`AttackIntent`

Represents a concrete melee target. It is consumed by [`combatSystem`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/rules/systems/combatSystem.js:736).

`AttackDirectionIntent`

An intermediate player-facing intent. The [`attackDirectionSystem`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/rules/systems/attackDirectionSystem.js:1) resolves direction, confirmation, target selection, and then creates `AttackIntent`.

That is a good pattern: one intent expresses the player’s request, then a canonical system translates it into the actual combat intent.

`CastSpellIntent`

Created by input, AI callbacks, channel completion, and policy AI. [`castSpellSystem`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/rules/systems/castSpellSystem.js:157) performs the real validation:

- spell identity;
- whether the actor knows it;
- target validity and visibility;
- mana/stamina/life cost;
- intelligence requirements;
- interruption and channeling rules;
- canonical spell execution.

The producers should not duplicate those rules.

`RangedAttackIntent`

Created by AI and input. [`rangedAttackSystem`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/rules/systems/rangedAttackSystem.js:57) handles equipment, ammo, range, line of sight, projectiles, damage, and cleanup.

Movement deliberately runs before ranged attacks, so a creature can move and then fire based on its new position.

`WaitIntent`

Consumed by [`waitSystem`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/rules/systems/waitSystem.js:1). It is not semantically neutral: it changes combat posture to guarded.

That is why #186 correctly says tactical `hold` cannot be represented as `WaitIntent`.

The remaining intents—drink, use, throw, apply, equip, pickup, engrave, pray, disarm, interact, search, posture, and so on—follow the same general pattern:

- a producer adds a component;
- validation may remove it;
- one canonical consumer resolves it;
- the consumer removes it.

Player input follows this model in [`rulesDispatch.js`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/main/input/rulesDispatch.js:1). It adds one intent and immediately advances the world.

### Where validation fits

[`intentValidationSystem.js`](/home/pjensen/Repos/JSHack/src/rules/systems/intentValidationSystem.js:1) runs first in the intents phase.

It strips or cancels intents for:

- dead actors;
- sleeping actors;
- stunned actors;
- stasis;
- rooted actors;
- channeling actors;
- other status restrictions.

The list of recognized action intents is manually maintained in `ALL_INTENTS`.

### The obvious weaknesses

The biggest weakness is that most intents are requests without ownership metadata.

A `MoveIntent` does not say:

- who created it;
- why it exists;
- whether it is authoritative;
- whether it may be replaced;
- how long it remains valid;
- whether it represents pursuit, retreat, flanking, or holding.

Since ECS components are generally singleton-per-entity, a second writer either:

- fails to add the component;
- removes and replaces it;
- mutates it;
- or silently loses because the error is swallowed.

There are many examples of this pattern:

- `aiScurrySystem`;
- `aiChaseSystem`;
- social aggro;
- summoned behavior;
- townfolk AI;
- pet behavior;
- policy AI;
- taunt steering.

This is exactly why the chase/policy conflict became ambiguous. The movement component was being forced to carry semantics it was never designed to represent.

The second weakness is that scheduler registration order is not sufficient. We discovered that the actual AI order did not initially match the comments: the tactical resolver ran before chase and policy. Explicit `before`/`after` constraints are necessary for behavior ownership.

The third weakness is incomplete action exclusivity. An actor can potentially possess multiple action intents at once—for example movement plus casting—unless a particular producer or resolver removes one. There is no universal “this actor has selected one action for this turn” arbitration layer. The tactical resolver now handles that for its migrated path, but the general system does not.

The fourth weakness is the manually maintained validation list. Adding a new action intent requires remembering to update:

- `ALL_INTENTS`;
- stunned restrictions;
- stasis restrictions;
- rooted restrictions;
- channeling restrictions;
- scheduler registration;
- the canonical consumer.

That is manageable, but easy to get wrong.

The fifth weakness is silent failure. Many producers use patterns like:

```js
try {
  world.add(actor, MoveIntent, data);
} catch {}
```

That prevents crashes, but it also hides ownership conflicts. In AI code, a swallowed duplicate often means “another system won,” which is gameplay-significant information.

### Where `TacticalIntent` improves things

The new component creates a distinction between:

- objective: `flank`, `hold`, `pursue`, `retreat`, etc.;
- action: `MoveIntent`, `CastSpellIntent`, `RangedAttackIntent`.

That gives us a proper ownership boundary:

```text
chase / policy
      ↓
TacticalIntent arbitration
      ↓
canonical action intent
      ↓
action system
```

The current implementation is a transitional rung because `aiChaseSystem` still emits a compatibility `MoveIntent` alongside the tactical objective. The clean end state should remove that direct write, migrate the remaining AI producers, and make the tactical resolver the sole movement projection point.

So the obvious next weakness is not the existence of the component—it is the remaining legacy movement writers that bypass it.

