content.text:
Yes. Three ordinary goblins against a lightly equipped player should create an action-economy problem: you should be forced to spend health, position, or a resource to survive.

I’d tune this in this order:

1. Make the pack reliably reach melee pressure.

The group should have one direct pursuer and two lateral approaches, but all three must continue reducing distance. The spread constraint fixed the worst orbiting behavior; next we should verify that at least two goblins can attack simultaneously once contact occurs.

2. Stop the whole pack from spending turns on the same ability pattern.

Dirty Trick should be a tactical accent, not the group’s default action. A simple first experiment would be a per-pack ability budget: one goblin uses Dirty Trick while the others continue closing or attacking. That gives the player a meaningful blind/disable moment without removing the pack’s melee pressure.

3. Preserve ranged and disabling tools as legitimate answers, but make them costly.

Ranged killing all three is not automatically wrong if it costs ammunition, time, and positioning. The concern is that the player currently gets nine uncontested shots while the pack only deals 11 damage. We should measure:

- turns until first goblin contact;
- number of goblin attack events;
- player HP lost;
- ammo/resources spent;
- goblins alive at turns 8, 12, and 20.

4. Calibrate against an actually bare player.

The current generated probe still uses class loadouts, so the next baseline should remove armor and strong weapons. That will tell us whether three goblins are dangerous because of encounter tactics or merely because the player’s equipment dominates the test.

My recommended first gameplay experiment is therefore:

- keep damage scalar untouched;
- ensure two goblins continue melee pursuit;
- limit Dirty Trick to one active user in the pack;
- rerun bare-player melee, ranged, and disabling scenarios over multiple seeds.

If three goblins still fail to threaten the bare player after that, then we can tune attack cadence or authored goblin damage—not global damage.

